  "hints": [
    "Every path is a walk from the root; the only state you need per node is whether it is a directory and which names live directly under it.",
    "Insert one segment at a time, creating missing segments as directories, but check the whole path first because a segment that already exists as a file makes the entry invalid.",
    "For the listing, gather the immediate children of the queried node, sort the bare names, and only then add a trailing slash to the directories."
  ],
  "editorial": {
    "approach": "The brute force stores a flat dictionary from the full path string to a flag saying whether that node is a file or a directory. To test or create a prefix it rebuilds the prefix by joining the first k segments on every step, which is O(d) work per step and O(d^2) per path. Answering the query then scans every stored key, keeps the ones beginning with the queried prefix, and cuts the first remaining segment to get a child name. The optimal solution builds the same hierarchy once as a trie: each segment is one hash map lookup, every node owns its children, and the listing is a single iteration over the queried node's children followed by a sort.",
    "why_optimal": "Every input path must be read and its segments walked, which is Omega(n * d) for n paths of depth d, and the answer has to be sorted, which costs m log m comparisons for m children. The trie does exactly that much work and never touches a path that is not a prefix of the queried directory, while the flat dictionary re-derives prefixes and rescans the whole store at query time. With at most 150 paths of at most 40 characters the absolute difference is small, but the trie is the structure the problem is designed to exercise.",
    "pitfalls": "Forgetting that intermediate segments become directories automatically, so '/a/b/c.txt' must also create /a and /a/b. Letting a file become a directory or a directory become a file when the same name reappears with the other kind, and allowing a path to be created underneath a name that is already a file. Appending the slash before sorting instead of after, because a slash sorts before most name characters and can reorder entries such as 'a/' against 'a.b'. Handling the root in one branch but not the other, so a target of '/' or an empty target returns nothing instead of everything. Forgetting that leading and repeated slashes carry no meaning, and returning the children of a file instead of the empty string."
  },
